# Fixture and certificate bytes are normative.
#
# Every port hashes these files and compares the digest against the one
# fixtures/cases.json records, so the bytes have to be identical on every
# checkout. Git's default line-ending translation rewrites a text file on a
# Windows working copy, which changes those bytes and fails the registry
# check for anything it touches. It already did: the Go and .NET suites both
# failed on windows-latest over fixtures/public-receipts/LICENSE-upstream,
# and nowhere else.
#
# -text disables that translation. It is not about whether a file looks like
# text; it is about whether its bytes are load-bearing. These are.
fixtures/** -text
certs/** -text
*.der -text
*.cer -text
*.b64 -text
*.jws -text
*.hex -text

# What a Composer install of this repository receives.
#
# Packagist reads composer.json from a repository root and nowhere else, so
# the PHP package is the root manifest (BOOTSTRAP.md, "Packagist (PHP)",
# layout A). Composer then installs GitHub's zipball of a tag, and a zipball
# is a `git archive`, so these rules are the package's file list: whatever
# survives them is what every PHP consumer unpacks into vendor/.
#
# This is an ALLOWLIST — `* export-ignore` drops everything, and each shipped
# path is named back in. The alternative, naming the ports to exclude, was
# rejected because its failure mode is silent and permanent: a tenth port
# directory added next year matches no exclusion and ships inside the PHP
# package forever, and nothing about adding a port would ever point at this
# file. The allowlist fails the other way. Something the package needs and
# nobody named here goes MISSING, which is louder, and which
# tools/check-php-package.mjs turns into a red build: it lists the archive on
# every CI run and fails when php/src, php/certs/*.cer or composer.json is
# absent, or when anything outside the list is present.
#
# Directories have to be named before their contents. `git archive` prunes an
# export-ignored directory without reading its children, so `/php` and
# `/php/src` are un-ignored in their own right, not only through the `**`
# rule underneath them.
#
# The cost, which is real and applies to more than Composer: GitHub builds
# the "Source code (zip)" asset on every Release from the same archive, so
# that asset is now the PHP package rather than the whole monorepo. Nothing
# in .github/workflows/ consumes it. Every other registry publishes a built
# artifact, and SwiftPM and the Go module proxy clone the repository instead
# of downloading an archive, so none of them see these rules.
* export-ignore
/composer.json -export-ignore
/LICENSE -export-ignore
/php -export-ignore
/php/composer.json -export-ignore
/php/LICENSE -export-ignore
/php/README.md -export-ignore
/php/certs -export-ignore
/php/certs/** -export-ignore
/php/src -export-ignore
/php/src/** -export-ignore
